st` nor `run_oneclick_preflight` ever validated SL/TP legs against the Mode-A grid `SweepGridConfig.__post_init__` (ADR-0007 condition 3) requires under `stage3_enable=True` — so `sl="none"` (accepted by `_normalise_leg`, and the OLD light-research default's own shape before this PR's flip — with an EXISTING regression test, `test_explicit_none_leg_passes_through`, proving it "passed through" cleanly) or any off-Mode-A-grid paisa value passed the ENTIRE R4/R20 preflight with `ok: true` on BOTH the one-click routes AND the legacy `/api/v1/sweep/run` route (which has no preflight step at all), launched, and then failed deterministically inside `cmd_sweep_all` with the real reason visible only in a background-thread stderr print — directly contradicting the explicit "a run that starts is provably runnable" (R16 layer 2) design claim this same diff's `app.py` docstring makes. Live-reproduced end-to-end via a real `TestClient` (`sl=51&tp=105` and `sl=none&tp=none` both preflight-`ok:true` then launch-then-fail). Fixed by adding a new `_validate_mode_a_legs` check inside `resolve_sweep_request` (used by every entry point — both one-click routes via `resolve_oneclick_sweep_plan`, and the legacy route directly) that normalizes each SL/TP paisa value against `brutex.engine.stage3_grid.MODE_A_SL_AXIS`/`MODE_A_TP_AXIS.normalize()` and raises a clear `ValueError` (the SAME 400-mapping convention already used for out-of-scope symbols) for a `"none"` or off-grid leg — refusing loudly at request-resolution time, before ANY background job starts, rather than silently reporting `ok: true` for an unrunnable launch. The pre-existing `test_explicit_none_leg_passes_through` test (asserting the NOW-WRONG old behavior) was rewritten to `test_explicit_none_leg_now_refused`, pinning the fix; `test_custom_values_are_normalised` (which used off-grid SL/TP values incidentally) was updated to on-grid values; two new tests (`test_off_grid_sl_now_refused`, `test_off_grid_tp_now_refused`) pin the off-grid-value refusal shape. This round was NOT clean (the most significant real bug of the whole review was found), so the "two consecutive clean passes" counter resets to zero; at least two more consecutive clean rounds are needed before the adversarial-refuter protocol is considered satisfied — this is flagged honestly, not silently claimed as done, and is the state this ledger entry ships in. Re-verified 100.00% line+branch (171 tests, `sweep_runner.py` now 196/48 stmts/branches) + `mypy --strict`/`pyright`/`ruff` clean after this fix; the full repo test suite was also re-run to confirm no collateral breakage in any other caller of `resolve_sweep_request`. **TWELFTH ADVERSARIAL-REFUTER ROUND (1-agent, this same PR, after the round above):** confirmed the round-11 SL/TP fix closes the gap comprehensively across all 3 entry points (legacy `/api/v1/sweep/run` directly, both one-click routes via `resolve_oneclick_sweep_plan`'s per-exchange-group calls — including the BSE/SENSEX fan-out group, not just the NSE default) via live `TestClient` runs, and mutation-tested the round-11 tests (all correctly fail when `_validate_mode_a_legs`'s call site is reverted). Then, deliberately looking for a SYMMETRIC gap on a DIFFERENT axis, found the SAME "preflight says ok, engine construction says no" failure class round 11 fixed for SL/TP also applies to `k`: `_normalise_k` only checked `k` was in the raw `[1, TOTAL_CONDITIONS]` RANGE, never against the engine's OWN `max_combos` guard (`SweepGridConfig.__post_init__`) — since `build_sweep_kwargs` always uses the full 60-bit vocabulary (`conditions=None`) and the engine default `max_combos=100_000`, a single `k=4` alone enumerates `C(60,4)=487,635` masks, far past the cap; this is a `tuple_count=1` scenario too small for the SLA wall-clock guard to ever catch, and the preflight's OWN estimator correctly reports the exact over-cap trial count while still returning `ok: true`. Also found the legacy `/api/v1/sweep/run` route's background `_work` returned `cmd_sweep_all`'s raw exit code with NO check at all (unlike the multi-exchange one-click route's `_work`, hardened across rounds 5-8) — any internal `cmd_sweep_all` config-error path (including the `k`/`max_combos` case) was silently reported as job state "done" with `on_success`/`mark_dirty` firing on a run that did nothing, a false-success signal on a still-reachable route. Fixed both: a new `_validate_k_against_max_combos` check inside `_normalise_k` (the same 400-before-any-launch pattern as round 11) refuses a `k` whose mask count exceeds `DEFAULT_MAX_COMBOS`; the legacy route's `_work` now checks the exit code, returns a named `{"note": ...}` finish detail on failure (mirroring the one-click route's established contract), and gates `on_success` on an actual success flag. Two pre-existing tests (`test_k_bound_is_the_full_60_vocabulary`, `test_custom_values_are_normalised`) that incidentally used over-cap `k` values were updated to reflect the new, correct refusal; four new tests pin both fixes (`test_k_over_max_combos_now_refused`, `test_k_within_max_combos_still_accepted`, `test_nonzero_exit_code_surfaces_a_failure_not_a_false_done`, `test_nonzero_exit_code_does_not_mark_cache_dirty`). This round was NOT clean (two more real gaps found, on top of round 11's fix in the same general area), so the "two consecutive clean passes" counter resets to zero again; at least two more consecutive clean rounds are needed before the adversarial-refuter protocol is considered satisfied. Re-verified 100.00% line+branch (173 tests, `sweep_runner.py` now 205/52 stmts/branches) + `mypy --strict`/`pyright`/`ruff` clean after every fix above. ENGINE UNCHANGED — NIFTY/BANKNIFTY/SENSEX (+ ADR-0012 NIFTY-futures track); no vocabulary/rate-cap/lake-schema/audit-schema change; the institutional default's SL/TP grid is a NEW paisa choice (`50/100/150` × `100/200/300`), never previously swept, so no existing run identity moves. **Decision ID:** DEC-SWEEP-ONECLICK-PREFLIGHT-ESTIMATOR-001. **Builds on:** the operator's one-click institutional sweep directive (parent programme, tracked operator/coordinator-side — not yet its own ledger entry), DEC-SWEEP-PREFLIGHT-001 (#663, the G1 gate this reuses), DEC-SLA-LAUNCH-GUARD-001 (`sweep_sla_guard.py`), DEC-FUTURES-EXEC-TRANSLATION-001 (#1543, P5's shipped preflight this calls), DEC-ENGINE-SCOPE-GUARD-001 (`sweep_scope.py`, unedited, composed via the fan-out). **R54 none-leg reconcile addendum (2026-07-09, per the binding operator ruling 2026-07-08 — "the sweep must cover BOTH SL/TP arms AND no-SL/TP arms; `none` legs stay first-class RUNNABLE — warn or config exemption OK, a hard refusal is NOT; institutional defaults may remain the DEFAULT; document the reconcile in this ledger entry"):** the round-11 none-arm hard refusal in `brutex/dashboard/sweep_runner.py` is RELAXED — `_normalise_leg` now accepts the CLI `_parse_paisa_list` grammar PER VALUE (the whole-`none` leg AND the mixed both-arms-in-one-sweep shape `sl="none,5,100,200"`, PR #1297's default grid string, which the whole-string check had hard-refused with a self-contradictory error); the NEW `leg_values`/`leg_has_none` helpers are the ONE parsed-leg source of truth (CLI-grammar parity test-pinned) feeding the derived `SweepRunRequest.stage3_enable` — ANY `none` cell downgrades the launch's EFFECTIVE `stage3_enable` to False (the CLI's own default posture; CMA-ES cannot seed its x0 from a `none` cell, ADR-0007 condition 3; with Stage-3 off the engine accepts `None` cells, so the launch stays provably runnable — R16 preserved, engine constructibility test-pinned) — and the Mode-A off-grid refusal now applies ONLY under the effective stage3-ON posture (a `none`-carrying launch had just DISABLED the sole constraint that refusal protects — refusing e.g. `sl=none&tp=1500` was refusing a provably runnable launch on a disabled justification; fully-gridded launches keep the round-11 refusal byte-intact, the deep-crash class stays closed). The downgrade is DISCLOSED on every operator surface, never server-log-only: `stage3_enable` rides the request echo (the `POST /api/v1/sweep/run` response payload), the one-click preflight verdict carries ONE `stage3_enable` requested→applied Clamp row per downgraded exchange group (the operator proceed-dialog's own data channel; the reason states honestly that a mixed launch's GRIDDED cells lose Stage-3 CMA-ES refinement too), and `build_sweep_kwargs` emits ONE `sweep_runner.none_leg_stage3_downgrade` WARN per BUILT engine-run kwargs at LAUNCH time — so a refused request or the read-only preflight never logs a false "downgraded OFF for this launch" claim, and the multiplicity claim is honest (one per launched exchange-group engine run). Institutional defaults stay the DEFAULT (`DEFAULT_SL`/`DEFAULT_TP` unchanged — the ruling's explicit allowance); route-level R54 pins land on BOTH launch surfaces (`/sweep/run` + the one-click preflight/run pair) so a route-side re-validation cannot silently re-violate the ruling. ENGINE UNCHANGED — NIFTY/BANKNIFTY/SENSEX; no scope/vocab/rate change; `sweep_runner.py` re-verified 100.00% line+branch.
 1630. **DEC-SWEEP-LOG-VOLUME-GUARD-001 — the sweep CLI's structlog output is BOUNDED at INFO by default (fail-loud `$BRUTEX_LOG_LEVEL` override): a bare `brutex sweep`/`sweep-all`/`sweep-daemon` process left structlog UNCONFIGURED (the default wrapper class filters at NOTSET), so the engine's per-trade DEBUG events (`trade_sim.entry`/`trade_sim.exit` — two per simulated trade) streamed unfiltered to stderr — a MEASURED 870 MB of log output on a ~200-session composite-e2e fixture, which on the §8 production 2-4 day full-grid run is a disk-fill risk (disk-full mid-sweep → `LakeWriteHalt` = a run-killer) plus severe I/O overhead** (2026-07-08 on `claude/sweep-log-level-guard`; fix(cli)). THE FIX (minimal + §11 COMMON): ONE dispatch-level guard in `cli._dispatch` — `args.command ∈ {sweep, sweep-all, sweep-daemon}` → the NEW `cli._configure_sweep_logging()` — routing through the EXISTING ONE COMMON `brutex.observability.logging.configure_logging` seam (the same seam `cmd_dashboard` configures at startup; ADR-0005) at INFO/console-renderer, at the `main()` dispatch BEFORE any engine module is even imported, so every engine logger's first use binds the filtering wrapper (structlog caches on first use); `sweep-daemon` inherits doubly (guard at its own dispatch + it reuses `cmd_sweep_all`). Env resolution is the NEW `observability/logging.resolve_env_log_level` (the env wrapper that module's own design contract prescribes — the core function stays env-free): unset/blank → default INFO; `DEBUG`/`INFO`/`WARNING`/`ERROR` accepted case-insensitively; ANYTHING else — including `CRITICAL`, since silencing ERROR would hide halts — raises → `CliUserError` → one `[brutex] invalid BRUTEX_LOG_LEVEL=…` line + exit 1 with the sweep handler NEVER invoked (junk is never a silent fallback). DEFER RULE: when `structlog.is_configured()` the guard validates the env var then no-ops — the dashboard's in-process sweep job (`run_sweep_from_request` → `cmd_sweep_all`) keeps `cmd_dashboard`'s INFO + rotating-file-sink configuration (reconfiguring would DROP that file sink, and structlog's first-use logger cache would ignore a mid-process swap anyway); that path was ALREADY bounded at INFO — the bare CLI process was the unbounded one. HONEST (Golden Rule #10): bounds LOG VOLUME ONLY — a sink-level threshold; NO event's level is changed and nothing at WARNING/ERROR is dropped (test-pinned); the hash-chained audit JSONL (Golden Rule #8 — `engine.sweep_audit.SweepAuditWriter` + `orchestrator.audit.AuditWriter`) is written by direct file appends, NOT structlog events, so audit records are byte-untouched at every level (pinned: neither module imports structlog); the 870 MB figure is the composite-run FIXTURE measurement — no production-run log size is claimed; `BRUTEX_LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG` deliberately restores the full per-trade stream (the operator's diagnosis lever, disk cost re-accepted explicitly). TESTS (each captures ACTUAL stderr bytes via capsys, never a sink mock): default suppresses a per-trade-shaped DEBUG event / INFO+WARNING+ERROR still emit at default / `DEBUG` env restores / junk fails loud pre-handler (fresh AND already-configured process) / all three sweep entries pass the ONE guard / a non-sweep command (`version`) stays logging-untouched / an existing configuration is deferred to / the audit-writers-are-structlog-free honesty pin — NEW `tests/unit/test_cli_sweep_log_level.py` (13) + `TestResolveEnvLogLevel` (13) in `tests/unit/test_observability_logging.py`; the six existing test files that drive `main([… "sweep*" …])` for OTHER behavior opt into ONE shared conftest fixture (`_inert_sweep_logging_guard`, module-level `usefixtures` mark — Golden Rule #11, no six-copy fixture) so no process-global logging config leaks between tests. R4 REVIEW FOLD (refuter-confirmed, same branch pre-merge): the dispatch guard alone covered ONLY the PARENT process — the production sweep is PARALLEL (`run_parallel_grid` builds a `ProcessPoolExecutor(mp_context=spawn, initializer=_worker_init)`), a SPAWN worker starts from a CLEAN interpreter (the parent's structlog config is NOT inherited), and the per-trade DEBUG events fire INSIDE the workers onto the inherited stderr fds — so the disk-fill stream was UNFILTERED on exactly the parallel path. FIX: `_worker_init` now applies the SAME bound worker-side via the NEW `observability/logging.configure_process_logging_best_effort` (`$BRUTEX_LOG_LEVEL` propagates to spawn children via the environment; junk falls back to INFO instead of raising — the parent entries already failed loud PRE-pool; defer-if-configured; ANY config failure swallowed — a worker must NEVER die over logging, best-effort by contract). REAL spawn regression net (`TestWorkerLogVolumeGuardSpawn`, capfd fd-level capture of actual child output through a REAL 2-worker `run_parallel_grid`): `trade_sim.*` ABSENT at default / `trade_sim.entry`+`exit` PRESENT with `BRUTEX_LOG_LEVEL=DEBUG` (the DEBUG twin proves the absence assertion non-vacuous) + in-process `_worker_init` binding/never-breaks pins + 5 direct helper-branch tests. The dashboard-launched sweep inherits the worker fix automatically (`run_sweep_from_request` → `cmd_sweep_all` → the same `run_parallel_grid` → `_worker_init`). MERGE-TO-MAIN FIX (folded when rebasing onto current main via a merge commit, same branch pre-merge): merging `origin/main` surfaced a TEST-ISOLATION leak the in-process worker/CLI logging config introduces — an in-process `_worker_init` / `_configure_sweep_logging` call configures structlog with `cache_logger_on_first_use=True`, freezing a shared module logger (e.g. `engine.sweep_run._LOG`) against the CURRENT processors list; the parallel suite's autouse `reset_configuration()` then swapped that list via `structlog.reset_defaults()`, ORPHANING the frozen proxy, so a LATER test's `structlog.testing.capture_logs` (which captures by mutating the current config's processors list IN PLACE, "to not break references held by bound loggers") could no longer observe that proxy — its INFO events silently bypassed the sink. That failed 9 `test_engine_sweep_run*.py` path-observability tests (`sweep_run.trade_sim_path` / `trade_sim_chunk_failed` / `bounded_rank_engaged` — ALL INFO events that DO emit under the bound, NOT DEBUG-suppression) with `len(events)==0` / `IndexError` when the `test_engine_*.py` coverage gate ran the files together (each passed alone). FIX (test-support only — the production log-volume bound is byte-untouched): `observability/logging.reset_configuration` now also clears structlog's first-use logger cache (NEW `_clear_structlog_first_use_cache` — resolves the proxy class via `type(structlog.get_logger())`, drops ONLY the overridden `bind` INSTANCE attribute on live `BoundLoggerLazyProxy` objects, never `_logger`, so each proxy re-assembles against the CURRENT config on next use), making the COMMON test-only reset a TRUE reset. Regression-pinned by `test_reset_unfreezes_capture_logs_for_module_loggers` (freeze → reset → `capture_logs` observes the event; 0 without the clear) and proven against the exact CI `tests/unit/test_engine_*.py` glob (was 1956 passed + 9 failed → 1965 passed). NO production path calls `reset_configuration`; the sweep INFO-default bound + the `_worker_init` worker bound are unchanged. OPERATOR NOTE (documented in the guard docstring): `BRUTEX_LOG_LEVEL=ERROR` also silences the WARNING-level operational events (stall-watchdog / quarantine warnings) an unattended launch-night sweep relies on — INFO (default) or WARNING recommended; ERROR is an opt-in hazard. `brutex/engine/sweep_parallel.py` stays 100.00% line+branch (its own 87-test suite). **Numbering:** takes #1630 — the next free number at/above the allocated floor (live-main max #1597 after merging origin/main; #1610–#1628 reserved/taken by in-flight PRs; the gap is legal — uniqueness is the law, DEC-LEDGER-RENUMBER-001), re-verified against the merged file before push. ENGINE UNCHANGED (NIFTY/BANKNIFTY/SENSEX), F&O lake REFERENCE_ONLY, no scope/vocab/rate change; `brutex/cli.py` (1173 stmts/264 branches) + `brutex/observability/logging.py` (114/24, incl. the merge-fix cache clear) BOTH 100.00% line+branch via the discovery gate; ruff + ruff-format clean; `mypy --strict` clean on both touched source files. **Files:** brutex/cli.py, brutex/observability/logging.py, brutex/engine/sweep_parallel.py, tests/conftest.py, tests/unit/{test_cli_sweep_log_level,test_observability_logging,test_engine_sweep_parallel,test_cli_user_error_hygiene,test_cli_sweep_launch_wiring,test_cli_backfill}.py, tests/integration/{test_cli_sweep,test_cli_sweep_scope,test_cli_sweep_scope_guard}.py, docs/MIGRATION.md, CLAUDE.md. **Decision ID:** DEC-SWEEP-LOG-VOLUME-GUARD-001.
 1561. **DEC-OPERATOR-PAGE-REAL-001 — the Operator page (`/`) is wired to the sweep-oneclick ARTIFACT CONTRACTS and the v29 PREVIEW mockup is KILLED by a MECHANICAL sweep; NEW `/screener` + `/strategy` pages; the Run button MOVES to the Operator page; the R44 VERIFY-ME panel ships with a 5-state truth table — DISPLAY ZONE ONLY** (2026-07-07 on `claude/quirky-pascal-70oduy`; dashboard display + static JS + templates + tests + docs — ZERO engine/orchestrator/ranking/results-storage/preflight edits, sweep/cascade POST endpoints byte-unchanged; ENGINE UNCHANGED — NIFTY/BANKNIFTY/SENSEX, F&O lake REFERENCE_ONLY, no scope/vocab/rate change). **THE KILL SWEEP (C-B1, mechanical not hand-listed):** every hardcoded ₹/count/date/invented-strategy-name site in v29.html was grep-inventoried and re-fed from a contract field (`data-oc=` hooks), em-dashed under the loud `#ocAwaiting` banner, or DELETED where no contract source exists — the fabricated CHARGES decomposition deleted outright (Track 1 is signal-only, CLAUDE.md §7 "Brokerage assumption: NONE"), the reel overlay / podium / rule cards / manual-slider SIMULATED counts deleted wholesale (their 3 innerHTML sinks died with them — v29 sinks 10 → 3, all pre-existing static-text toggles), and a generic zero-₹-outside-allowlist pin guards the class (v29-scoped). **THE INTEGRATION SEAM:** real funnel/badges/rank-1 card fed from `<lake>/sweeps/runs/<run_id>/` {manifest.json, top100.json, events.jsonl} through ONE clearly-marked adapter (`brutex/dashboard/oneclick_artifacts.py`) + pure payload builders (`brutex/dashboard/oneclick_view.py`) — LOUD "awaiting real run artifact" states, named corrupt/partial/EMPTY degrades (incl. a valid-but-empty top100 = status ok, `row_count` always `len(rows)` never an assumed 100), NEVER a 500 or a fabricated count; trades ride a typed `TradesReader` seam whose PRODUCTION default is the honest `AwaitingTradesReader` — the file-reading `FixtureTradesReader` activates ONLY under the fail-loud `BRUTEX