 1608. **DEC-SWEEP-STALL-OOM-GUARDS-001 — the parallel grid-sweep gains a HEAVY-GRID-AWARE stall watchdog + a distinct BrokenProcessPool QUARANTINE, closing two round-3 meta-refuter run-killers: (1) a legitimately-slow k=5 1-minute tuple no longer false-aborts a multi-hour grid near completion, and (2) a worker OOM no longer becomes a silent identical-resume livelock** (2026-07-08 on `claude/sweep-stall-oom-guards`; fix(engine); additive resilience — a CLEAN run is byte-identical, frozen sweep goldens + P-06/suffix-independence UNMODIFIED). **THE TWO DEFECTS (from the round-3 meta-refuter audit of `brutex/engine/sweep_parallel.py`):** (1, HIGH) the stall watchdog fired a flat `DEFAULT_STALL_SECONDS = 300.0` — but ONE k=5 1-minute NIFTY tuple (`C(60,5) = 5.46M` masks × hundreds of thousands of bars) runs as a single worker future FAR longer than 5 min with NO sub-tuple progress reported to the parent, so at the grid tail — when only slow heads remain in-flight and none COMPLETES within 300 s — `_await_next_result` raised `SweepStallError` → whole-run ABORT, killing a multi-hour grid near completion. (2, MED-HIGH) there was NO `concurrent.futures.process.BrokenProcessPool` handler: a k=5 worker OOM-killed while building `bar_bits`/finalists was caught by the generic `except Exception` → ABORT + re-raise, and because the checkpoint freezes at the last clean-prefix boundary an identically-configured resume re-dispatched the SAME deterministically-OOMing tuple → abort again = a silent no-progress loop across resumes. **THE FIXES (ONE change set, `brutex/engine/sweep_parallel.py` only — the SOLE source edit):** **(1) heavy-grid-aware stall bound** — each dispatched tuple's `mask·bar` work (`C(n_unique, k) × N_bars`, the exact per-tuple trade-sim work; `_tuple_work`) is stored per in-flight future in a new `_Dispatched` record; `_await_next_result` sizes the wait budget as `_effective_stall_seconds(max_inflight_work) = max(base_stall, work / STALL_MIN_MASK_BARS_PER_SEC)` so a tuple that legitimately needs N minutes gets a PROPORTIONAL bound while a light grid keeps the base floor byte-identically. The bound is FINITE, so a genuine no-progress hang is STILL caught (a wedged worker makes ZERO progress → its work-scaled deadline eventually elapses) — the guard is scaled, NOT disabled. `STALL_MIN_MASK_BARS_PER_SEC = 2.5e8` is a CONSERVATIVE hang-detection floor (Golden Rule #10: the resident Rust trade-walk is order-of-magnitude ~1e9 `mask·bar`/s, so 2.5e8 gives ~4× headroom against false-abort; it sets ONLY the false-abort-margin ↔ wedge-detection-latency trade-off, never results). Env knobs: `BRUTEX_SWEEP_STALL_SECONDS` (base floor) + `BRUTEX_SWEEP_STALL_MASK_BARS_PER_SEC` (throughput floor), both positive-float, unset→default, invalid→default+LOUD WARNING (`resolve_stall_seconds`/`resolve_stall_min_mask_bars_per_sec` over the shared `_resolve_positive_float_env`); the constructor's `stall_seconds`/`stall_min_mask_bars_per_sec` default to `None` → env-resolved, so `run_parallel_grid` (and the CLI) pick it up with no call-site change. **(2) OOM quarantine — durable, REVERSIBLE, visible** — `_drive` catches `BrokenProcessPool` DISTINCTLY (from `pool.submit` OR `future.result()` — `result()` runs BEFORE `pending.pop` so the broken future stays in `pending`), and `_await_next_result` catches a worker's surviving Python-level `MemoryError` (the cgroup/rlimit OOM shape — the pool does NOT break) as the single-tuple twin (`_quarantine_memory_error`). `_quarantine_broken_pool` first DRAINS already-FINISHED sibling futures (their REAL results apply — compute never discarded), then records every genuinely-broken in-flight tuple in the DURABLE quarantine sidecar `<root>/.sweep_quarantine.json` (fsync + atomic replace BEFORE anything else moves; `quarantine_sidecar_path`/`load_quarantined_tuple_labels`/`append_quarantine_entries` exported) and applies them as `"pool_broken"` outcomes — which deliberately do NOT `SweepCache.record` (that would perm